-# Development
-
-The README covers packing and playing. This page is for working on nxnative itself: how it is built, the script's options, running games on the Mac, and adding a game (per-game notes are in [dm/games](../dm/games/README.md#games)). Agent-oriented details (bring-up failure catalogue, graphics specifics, debugging recipes) are in [docs/agents](agents/README.md).
-
-## How it works
-
-| Part | State |
-|---|---|
-| Rewriter (`dm/tools/patch_nso.py`) | Runs unchanged on every module of every supported game. Per-build cuts are keyed by the module's build ID, in the profile's `patches.py` (a game's own modules) or `dm/tools/sdk_patches.py` (Nintendo's `sdk` modules). Profiles can opt verified cuts into [function matching](reference/rewriter.md#function-matched-cuts-for-unregistered-builds) across updates |
-| Host (`dm/host`) | Stands in for the Horizon kernel and the system services the games call: threads and synchronization, memory, file system with transactional saves, HID, audio, movies (VP9 through libvpx, Vorbis sound through stb_vorbis), software keyboard, focus and lifecycle |
-| Graphics (`dm/gfx`) | NVN on Metal. Ordinary and [tiled resolves](reference/rendering.md#tiled-multisample-resolves) share the recorded resolve path, including texture views. Shaders are extracted by `shader_split.py` from Nintendo's containers and from Unity's `Shader` objects, then translated offline. Mario: 16,266 of 16,268 shaders compile. Engage: 9,268 of 9,269. Games that render with Vulkan (Hades) or OpenGL (Digimon Story Cyber Sleuth) go through layers of their own on Metal (`dm/gfx/vk`, `dm/gfx/gl`); the GL layer also uploads [compressed 2D arrays](reference/rendering.md#compressed-opengl-texture-arrays) |
-| Audio | Games that carry the SDK's audio renderer (Mario) run it in-process; games that use AudioOut directly (Engage: Wwise) feed it straight. With Core Audio enabled (default on iOS, `DM_AUDIO=1` on the Mac), the [silent sink keeps releasing PCM buffers while a device starts or retries](reference/audio.md#a-blocked-audio-device-start-freezes-the-game). |
-| Input | iOS: the touch gamepad (hidden while a physical controller is connected) and physical controllers; shaking the phone shakes the controller (its six-axis sensor, `hle_hid.c`; the accelerometer in `DmGamepad.m`, threshold `DM_SHAKE_G`, 1.5 G by default). Mac: scripted input (`DM_KEYS=<seconds>:<button>,…`, `Shake` for a shake, with `DM_KBD=<text>` answering the system keyboard; `DM_REC=<file>` records what the player does in that syntax, [05](agents/05-debugging.md#scripted-input-and-thread-scheduling)) or `DM_WINDOW=1` for a window with keyboard input (Space shakes) and physical controllers (face buttons by position as on the iPhone, a Nintendo pad by its labels: `pad_apple.h`). On the Mac and iOS each controller is a player of its own, for local co-op: the first one connected is player 1, together with the keyboard (iOS: the touch gamepad and the phone's gyro), the next player 2, and so on up to 8 (`pad_slots.h`); a controller that is removed frees its player and gets it back when it reconnects, and the log names each one's player and npad (`controller 'Xbox Wireless Controller' connected: player 2 (npad 1)`). Player 1 is the Handheld npad in handheld mode and No1 docked, player n is No n; the game's supported npad ids decide how many play (`hle_hid.c`): a one-player game gets every controller as player 1, and a controller past a game's last player is not used. `DM_PAD_MERGE=1` makes every controller player 1, as before (on iOS from `Documents/env.txt`); `DM_KBD_PLAYER=own` makes the keyboard player 1 on its own, with the controllers from player 2 on. The window goes fullscreen from its green button or ⌃⌘F; `DM_FULLSCREEN=1` starts it that way. In fullscreen, a screen whose refresh rate is not a multiple of the game's (144 or 165 Hz) runs at one that is (120 Hz) until the window leaves fullscreen; `DM_SCREEN_MATCH=0` keeps its rate. The drawable follows the window in pixels (fullscreen at the screen's resolution); `DM_METAL_HUD=1` shows Apple's performance HUD (on the iPhone too, from `Documents/env.txt`). The left mouse button is the touchscreen, on the 16:9 picture. Motion: a controller with a gyroscope drives its player's six-axis sensor (`motion_apple.h` → `dm_motion_set`, `hle_hid.c`; orientation from the gyro with tilt correction toward gravity, `motion_fusion.h`), and on iOS the phone's own device motion drives player 1's while no controller supplies fresh motion (`DmGamepad.m`, in the Switch frame: the pad view's own frame on iOS 27 and later (`deviceMotionBody`), turned for the screen's orientation before that ([The phone's motion in the picture's frame](reference/services.md#the-phones-motion-in-the-pictures-frame)); `DM_PHONE_MOTION=0|1|auto`; `1` selects only the phone). On the Mac, a Switch Pro Controller, or a DualShock 4 over Bluetooth, whose GameController motion is empty can use passive HID reports instead (`motion_pro_mac.h`; `DM_MOTION_HID=0` turns this reader off). This requires exactly one pad of that model in each API so it can use the same player slot without guessing; multiple pads of a model retain GameController input. Raw HID has its own orientation and nominal sensor scales; hardware validation of the reworked reader remains open ([Pro Controller fallback](reference/services.md#switch-pro-controller-advertises-motion-but-sends-zeroes)). An original (Switch 1) Joy-Con has no motion through GameController at all, so on the Mac the host reads each Joy-Con wholly from its HID reports and leaves GameController's controllers of them unread (`pad_jc1_mac.h`: it sends the console's handshake, reads the IMU and stick calibrations over SPI, drives HD rumble and the player lights; a left and a right one make a pad, a lone one is held sideways, and each is a device of its own to the npad layer, so the game picks dual or single; [the reference entry](reference/services.md#an-original-joy-cons-sensors-are-read-by-the-host); `DM_JC1=0` gives them back to GameController, `DM_JC1_SIDE=L|R` picks the half that feeds the merged stream, `DM_JC1_LOG=1` logs the handshake and the calibrations, `=2` adds a frame line a second). `DM_MOTION_AXES` / `DM_MOTION_ACC_SIGN` apply only to GameController. Each source keeps its own orientation. Without live motion the sensor lies flat as before. `DM_MOTION_LOG=1` logs player 1's mapped sample once a second per source; `DM_MOTION_AXES` (e.g. `x,-z,y`) and `DM_MOTION_ACC_SIGN=-1` remap a controller whose axes differ, `DM_MOTION_GAIN` sets the tilt correction (0.5), `DM_MOTION=0` turns motion off (the NSP script's `--no-motion` builds it in). The console's own sensor (`SevenSixAxisSensor`, read in handheld mode) still reports no states. In handheld mode the touches also drive `hid::GetGestureStates` (taps, presses, pans, swipes, pinches and rotations recognized by the host, `hle_touch.c`) |
-| iOS app | One unsigned IPA for iPhone and iPad. The rewritten code is embedded in the executable; the shader libraries, the RomFS and the game icon are inside the app. A settings screen comes before the game (`Launcher.swift`, drawn from `dm/launcher.json`, which `dm/games/settings.py` writes from `dm/games/settings.tsv`); the host's loading starts at app launch meanwhile, and the frame rate and render scale go into the frame step state when the game starts (`loader.c` `dm_fps_state_apply`) |
-| Known gaps | Conditional render is always drawn; [indirect-count multi-draws](reference/rendering.md#indirect-count-multi-draws) can wait for a GPU-written count when the records need conversion, cannot be GPU-masked, or exceed the mask table capacity; geometry shaders do not translate. TBDR optimizations are off by default (they don't understand bindless textures yet). No amiibo. On the Mac, Mario's menu and dialogue text render blank (fine on the iPhone) |
-
-On the Mac, `DM_KEYMAP=<name>=<key code>,…` puts a button, a stick direction (the right stick too, on no key by default) or `Shake` on another key, and `DM_PAD_MAP=<name>=<name>,…` changes what a controller's buttons send (`A=B,B=A` swaps them; `input_map.h`).
